Finite joins of elements, and singletons, are elements of the powerdomain.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinZero`).** The empty powerdomain join is the least powerdomain element.

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmemFinJoin`).** A powerdomain neighbourhood belongs to the finite join of \(x_i\) when there are neighbourhoods \(X_i\) of the respective \(x_i\) whose generated upper sets are all contained in it.

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinSucc`).** For a nonempty finite family, the preceding membership condition defines a powerdomain element.

*Proof.* The master neighbourhood is witnessed by master neighbourhoods of all components; intersections are witnessed componentwise; and membership is upward closed. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoin`).** The finite join is the least element for an empty family and the nonempty finite-join element otherwise.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_finJoinZero`).** A neighbourhood belongs to the empty finite join exactly when it is the master powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* This is the membership rule for the least element. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingleton`).** The singleton powerdomain element is the one-term finite join.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_finJoinSucc`).** Membership in a nonempty finite join is exactly the finite-join witness condition.

*Proof.* This is the defining membership relation. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_singleton`).** A powerdomain neighbourhood belongs to the singleton generated by \(x\) exactly when it contains the upper set of some neighbourhood of \(x\).

*Proof.* Specialize the nonempty finite-join condition to the unique index of a one-element family. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_finJoin`).** Membership in a finite join is equality with the master neighbourhood when the family is empty, and the finite-join witness condition otherwise.

*Proof.* Separate the zero and successor cases of the family size. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_finJoin_iUnion`).** For a nonempty family, membership in its finite join is equivalent to containing the union of the upper sets generated by suitable component neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Containing every generated upper set is equivalent to containing their union. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_down`).** A powerdomain neighbourhood is downward closed among original neighbourhoods: if it contains \(Z\), \(X\subseteq Z\), and \(X\) is a neighbourhood, then it contains \(X\).

*Proof.* Choose a generator whose upper set contains \(Z\); transitivity gives that the same generator's upper set contains \(X\). ∎
